      <h2 class="text-center section-title">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="faq-section">
        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>How long does tennis court construction take?</h3>
          <p>Typical residential tennis court construction takes 7-10 business days after excavation and base preparation. This includes surface installation, line marking, net installation, and final cleanup. Commercial projects may take 2-3 weeks depending on complexity.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>What's the difference between cushioned and non-cushioned surfaces?</h3>
          <p>Cushioned surfaces include shock-absorbing layers beneath the playing surface, reducing stress on players' joints and providing a more comfortable playing experience. Non-cushioned hard courts offer faster play and lower initial cost but may be less comfortable for extended play sessions.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>Can I add pickleball lines to my tennis court?</h3>
          <p>Absolutely! Tennis courts can accommodate multiple pickleball courts. We can add pickleball lines in contrasting colors while maintaining the tennis lines, creating a versatile multi-sport facility that maximizes your court's utility.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>How often does a tennis court need resurfacing?</h3>
          <p>With proper maintenance, tennis courts typically need resurfacing every 4-6 years. Factors like usage intensity, climate exposure, and maintenance quality affect the timeline. Regular cleaning and minor repairs can extend surface life significantly.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>What permits are required for tennis court construction?</h3>
          <p>Most municipalities require building permits for tennis court construction. We handle the permit application process and provide professional drawings and specifications. HOA approval may also be required in some communities.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

